      "speaker": "Mīrzā Muḥammad ʿAlī",
      "arabicLemma": "قوله ان أريد بجزء منه الدلالة على جزء معناه",
      "english": "The definition of a compound expression says that a part of its wording is *intended to signify a part of its meaning*. What kind of signification is meant? Al-Kātibī and others specify signification by correspondence (*muṭābaqa*); inclusion or implication alone is not enough. Otherwise a word might be compound under its whole assigned meaning while simple under a merely included or external associated meaning. Opponents reply that it is acceptable for one word to be simple in one linguistic assignment and compound in another, as in “ʿAbd Allāh” used as a proper name versus the possessive phrase “servant of God.”\n\nThe respondent distinguishes this familiar ambiguity across *different linguistic assignments* from the proposed competition of two kinds of signification under one and the same assignment. Giving priority to the meaning indicated by correspondence has a rationale: any composition recognised under a contained or implied sense can be related to the whole assigned sense, while composition of the whole need not require composition of each included or associated meaning. Others reverse this reasoning for simplicity, but the reply says that “compound” is a positive notion and “simple” a privative one; in a classification of this kind, the positive mark of composition has explanatory priority.\n\nA major objection concerns finite verbs. If a verb's meaning includes action, time and relation to an agent, do its material letters signify the action while its vocalic pattern signifies time, making an ordinary verb a compound expression? One answer distinguishes an audible, successively arranged part from a merely formal pattern or grammatical configuration; an inflectional pattern is not independently heard as a separate sequence of sound in the way two words are. But then hidden pronominal subjects in imperatives seem to be genuine meaning-bearing constituents without an independently audible component. The response allows an expression that is hearable *in principle* or mentally restored in the grammatical analysis, as “strike [you]!” includes an understood subject.\n\nThe glossator criticizes the answer: if the vocalic form is not a meaningful part of the verb at all, the earlier argument assumed what it must prove. A more coherent position, he claims, holds that the verb is a single conventional unit whose assigned meaning is a simple event-form abstracted from action, time and relation, rather than the aggregate of three separately designated constituents. Under that view the apparent objection to the compound/simple division does not arise in the same form.\n\nWhy, nevertheless, present “compound” before “simple” when the simple precedes the compound in nature? The answer invokes the contrast of possession and privation: “compound” affirms a positive feature and “simple” denotes its absence. Since the definition is made through this conceptual opposition, the positive notion may appropriately come first even if an elementary simple expression is naturally prior to an articulated compound.",
