      "english": "The relevant component of an expression is what can be heard and ordered in hearing, actually or in grammatical supposition. This avoids counting the purely formal grammatical pattern of a finite verb as a separate audible word, while still allowing an implicitly understood subject in an imperative to be restored in analysis. Yet a contrary view says that if form were no part of the verb as a sign at all, one would fail to explain how its form can determine its temporal meaning. Further difficulty arises from audible prefixes in present-tense verbs and elements marking mutual participation: their segments carry meaning but are not always treated as independently compound words. The logicians' concept of one simple term need not coincide in every case with the grammarian's classification of one morphologically inflected word.",
