      "speaker": "Mīrzā Muḥammad ʿAlī",
      "arabicLemma": "قوله الرابع ان يكون هذه الدلالة مرادة",
      "english": "Meaning-signification may occur even when the speaker does not intend it. A person familiar with an ambiguous word automatically apprehends several assigned senses when hearing it, even if the speaker intends only one. Why, then, make intention part of the definition of a compound expression? The qualification concerns the **intended signification of one part toward one constituent** in the use of the whole expression, not the psychological presence of every possible meaning.\n\nThe note reports an important dispute. Avicenna in the *Shifāʾ* is quoted as saying that a word does not signify by its mere sound; its signification is related to what the speaker intends. If the word *ʿayn* is intended for a spring it indicates the spring; if for a coin, it indicates that. Without any intention, some logicians hesitate to call an uninterpreted sound a word. Al-Ṭūsī is similarly cited for treating conventional verbal signification as attached to the speaker's intention of the utterance.\n\nLater commentators strongly oppose this. Knowledge of a lexical assignment ordinarily makes a hearer conceive the assigned meaning whether or not the speaker intends it. A person who knows several senses of *ʿayn* can mentally access each on hearing it, while a contextual indicator is needed to identify which one was intended. The glossator proposes that much of the dispute is verbal: one school calls only the passage to the *intended* meaning “signification,” the other calls the broader automatic passage to known assigned senses “signification.” Both sides acknowledge that understood meanings and a particular speaker's intention are not the same fact.",
      "status": "partial_translation",
